1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,575
Welcome to Module Thirty, and to the capstone.

2
00:00:03,575 --> 00:00:06,312
Organisms don't only fit their niches.

3
00:00:06,312 --> 00:00:07,412
They build them.

4
00:00:07,412 --> 00:00:09,250
Beavers dam streams.

5
00:00:09,250 --> 00:00:11,437
Earthworms remake the soil.

6
00:00:11,437 --> 00:00:15,037
And the changed environment then shapes selection on the builders.

7
00:00:15,037 --> 00:00:19,787
You know this as niche construction, a two-way fit worked out over generations.

8
00:00:19,787 --> 00:00:22,725
Your capstone is a niche-construction project.

9
00:00:22,725 --> 00:00:29,412
You'll reshape one corner of your scientific work so that AI agents fit into it, and so that the work fits them.

10
00:00:29,412 --> 00:00:31,987
Today is about choosing that corner well.

11
00:00:31,987 --> 00:00:37,225
The first guide is a physicist, Matthew Schwartz, writing on Anthropic's science blog.

12
00:00:37,225 --> 00:00:39,850
He describes an impedance mismatch.

13
00:00:39,850 --> 00:00:46,125
Two systems that each work fine, but are badly matched, so most of what one puts in never reaches the other.

14
00:00:46,125 --> 00:00:50,262
His point is that these models are good at science but are not scientists.

15
00:00:50,262 --> 00:00:56,962
They have huge breadth, strong coding, maths and statistics, and they read papers and data at machine speed.

16
00:00:56,962 --> 00:00:59,987
What they can't do yet is the deep conceptual part.

17
00:00:59,987 --> 00:01:08,587
So he stopped treating Claude like a graduate student and went looking for problems shaped to its strengths, problems where the answer can be checked.

18
00:01:08,587 --> 00:01:10,962
One of those problems was in your field.

19
00:01:10,962 --> 00:01:13,712
Neutral theory of biodiversity.

20
00:01:13,712 --> 00:01:21,775
An equation that had resisted solution at scale for twenty years was solved, and applied to the forest plot on Barro Colorado Island.

21
00:01:21,775 --> 00:01:23,325
Technically impressive.

22
00:01:23,325 --> 00:01:28,325
But when he showed it to an ecologist, the verdict was that most ecologists would shrug.

23
00:01:28,325 --> 00:01:30,925
They already knew neutral theory falls short.

24
00:01:30,925 --> 00:01:38,075
The ecologist's better idea was to study what's left after you subtract the neutral prediction, and that became real science.

25
00:01:38,075 --> 00:01:41,675
Schwartz says nearly every project went this way.

26
00:01:41,675 --> 00:01:43,700
The model supplied the horsepower.

27
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:45,687
The expert supplied the taste.

28
00:01:45,687 --> 00:01:50,200
The second guide comes from engineers who put agents into companies for a living.

29
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:52,800
Their rule is to audit before you automate.

30
00:01:52,800 --> 00:01:56,950
One client needed seventeen steps to pay a single invoice.

31
00:01:56,950 --> 00:02:01,150
After the audit it was seven, and agents were only one part of the fix.

32
00:02:01,150 --> 00:02:07,262
Their method is to map the process as it really happens, which is usually far longer than the version on paper.

33
00:02:07,262 --> 00:02:09,987
Then sort every step into four buckets.

34
00:02:09,987 --> 00:02:10,975
Delete it.

35
00:02:10,975 --> 00:02:12,900
Turn it into a simple rule.

36
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:14,225
Give it to an agent.

37
00:02:14,225 --> 00:02:15,825
Or keep a human on it.

38
00:02:15,825 --> 00:02:19,625
And measure before you build, so you can prove the change later.

39
00:02:19,625 --> 00:02:21,762
Here's where the metaphor breaks.

40
00:02:21,762 --> 00:02:24,637
Niche construction is slow and mutual.

41
00:02:24,637 --> 00:02:30,850
Automation is fast, and if the process is broken, it simply runs the broken process faster.

42
00:02:30,850 --> 00:02:36,750
In the lab, your coding agent will interview you about one workflow you really do, step by step.

43
00:02:36,750 --> 00:02:48,137
Then it sorts the steps, finds a checkable anchor for every agent step, plans your baseline measurements, and writes a one-page capstone spec that gives each of your earlier builds a job.

44
00:02:48,137 --> 00:02:55,675
Start with Part A. Choose one recurring workflow, and list the steps as you actually do them, including the annoying ones.

